WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.502
This query creates two follow-up timestamps.

00:00:03.502 --> 00:00:06.627
The original discharge time remains visible.

00:00:06.627 --> 00:00:10.792
Then PostgreSQL adds fourteen-day and thirty-day intervals.

00:00:10.792 --> 00:00:17.767
Keeping the original and derived times side by side makes the transformation easy to audit and explain.

00:00:17.767 --> 00:00:20.717
Choose temporal types based on meaning.

00:00:20.717 --> 00:00:23.802
DATE is for calendar dates without time of day.

00:00:23.802 --> 00:00:28.247
TIMESTAMP stores a date and time without a time-zone interpretation.

00:00:28.247 --> 00:00:33.012
TIMESTAMPTZ represents an instant in time and is usually safer for events that cross regions or

00:00:33.012 --> 00:00:35.410
daylight-saving changes.
